{"sgrw_34" "The design of this 81mm gun closely follows its Soviet counterpart, consisting of three main sections: the firing tube, support plate and a two-legged mount. It fires 3.5kg HE rounds via indirect trajectory. Firing is triggered by the pin inside the tube striking the primer of a descending shell."}
{"sgrw_42" "This 120mm mortar is a copy of the Soviet PM 38 mortar which is itself a copy of the French 120mm Brandt mortar. Use this totally original design of a mortar to rain down fire on your opponents indiscriminately."}
{"60mm_brandt_usa" "This useless 60mm mortar can't hit anything further than a stone's throw but it is effective in being concealed while throwing rocks and plastic dildos in the enemy's vague direction."}
{"81mm_brandt_usa" "The M1 mortar is an American 81 millimeter caliber mortar. It was based on the French Brandt mortar. The M1 mortar was used from World War II until the 1950s when it was replaced by the lighter and longer ranged M29 mortar."}
{"ml_3_inch_mk_ii" "The Ordnance ML 3-inch mortar is an British 3-inch (in reality, 81.2 millimeter) calibre mortar. It was a successor to the Stokes mortar and while having an unsatisfactory initial range compared to its German counterparts, the weapon was improved throughout the war and became an effective infantry mortar."}
{"4_2_inch_mortar_m_2" "The Ordnance ML 4.2-inch mortar was a British. rifled 4.2-inch (110 mm) mortar used during the Second World War. It entered service in 1941. Use this mortar when you really want the enemy to cease fire."}
